<p><a class="cimage" href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09311.jpg"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async"  src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09311-scaled.jpg" alt="" width="840" height="560" class="alignnone size-full wp-image-125606" srcset="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09311-scaled.jpg 2560w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09311-300x200.jpg 300w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09311-1024x683.jpg 1024w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09311-768x512.jpg 768w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09311-1536x1024.jpg 1536w, https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/DSC09311-2048x1365.jpg 2048w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 840px) 100vw, 840px" /></a></p>
<p>I was watching <a href="https://moving-pictures.ingebrigtsen.no/2026/11/06/the-four-seasons/" data-cached-time="2026-11-07T21:19:31" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/cache-2026-11-07-web.webp">The Four Seasons</a> yesterday, and there were a couple of actors I wondered who were.  I mean, not Alan Alda, but a couple of the others&#8230;  so I hit the <a shot href="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/2025/09/24/who-is-that-actor-on-the-screen-emacs-llm-fun-redux/">&#8220;Emacs, tell me who what is&#8221;</a> button on my remote, and nothing much happened.</p>
<p>So things had bitrotted once again, which isn&#8217;t weird &#8212; the code is a bit of a Rube Goldberg thing <a href="https://github.com/larsmagne/movie.el" data-cached-time="2026-11-07T21:19:41" data-cached-image="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/cache-2026-11-07-web-1.webp">which you can peruse here</a>, but I wouldn&#8217;t really recommend it.</p>
<p>But I&#8217;ve spent the entire day fixing it, so I deserve to complain aimlessly in a blog post, right?</p>
<p>OK?</p>
<p>OK: The main problem is that AI web scrapers are hitting imdb so hard that Amazon has tightened its shield even more.  The main motor behind the Actor Guessing Machine is an LLM, and that API still works (if you can call &#8220;being wrong half of the time&#8221; to &#8220;work&#8221;), but I&#8217;m trying to use imdb to show the actor&#8217;s face (see inset on the top right above) so that I can check whether what the LLM said is likely.</p>
<p>My first idea was to just avoid using imdb altogether.  Instead I asked the LLM to include an image of the actor, too.  Shouldn&#8217;t be too hard, right?  Well, Gemini will do this if you ask &#8212; but it takes 45 seconds or so.  Yes!  45 seconds!  So that&#8217;s not very UX-ey.</p>
<p>I also tried using Claude, but it sternly told me that I was very naughty for even trying to make it output images of real people, and that I should be ashamed of myself.  Oh, and then also (since I had asked it to return JSON and nothing else), it also included this in the middle of the rant:</p>
<pre>```json
[]
```</pre>
<p><img src="https://s.w.org/images/core/emoji/17.0.2/72x72/1f937.png" alt="🤷" class="wp-smiley" style="height: 1em; max-height: 1em;" /></p>
<p>So&#8230;  Back to imdb:  I&#8217;m already using a headless Chrome to fetch imdb data, but now it requires popping up Chrome (once in a while) to click on some hats or something:</p>
<p><video controls loop poster="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/postermb1nqX001.jpg"><source src="https://lars.ingebrigtsen.no/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/video-7AYWfp.mp4" type="video/mp4"></video></p>
<p>How elegant!  I&#8217;m so proud of the UX!</p>
<p>So what I&#8217;m doing is running a &#8220;server&#8221; (Emacs based, of course) on the laptop, and when the TV computer needs to get some imdb data, it (Emacs) asks the laptop (Emacs), and if it then turns out that it needs to pop up a window, it can do so (Chrome).</p>
<p>*sigh*</p>
<p>So&#8230;  it&#8217;s all a bit stupid.  And is it worth it?  Eh.  It&#8217;s probably gonna break in a few weeks anyway.  I guess I&#8217;m more offended that I have to do this much work more than anything else.  The challenge?  Eh.  Dunno.</p>
<p>But when it doesn&#8217;t need a popup, it&#8217;s kinda OK:</p>
